## Claim
Text: "Someone who couldn't afford health care under Barack Obama's Affordable Care Act gets penalized $10,000"
Claim author: Capitalism
Claim date: October 20, 2020
Claim origin: https://web.archive.org/web/20210116144247/https://www.facebook.com/Capitalists/photos/a.226491315514/10164464788875515

## Initial Q&A
### What was the exact penalty amount for not having health insurance under the Affordable Care Act (ACA) for the 2020 tax year?
Answer: For the 2020 tax year, the penalty for not having health insurance under the Affordable Care Act was $0, as the individual mandate penalty was reduced to zero for years after 2018. The source states: "the individual mandate penalty has been reduced to zero for years after 2018" and "beginning in 2019, the government will no longer attempt to collect the individual mandate penalties."

Source URL: https://www.hollandhart.com/2019-repeal-of-the-affordable-care-acts-individual-mandate-penalties

### Did the Affordable Care Act provide exemptions from the individual mandate penalty for individuals who could not afford health insurance?
Answer: Yes, the Affordable Care Act provided exemptions from the individual mandate penalty for individuals who could not afford health insurance. Specifically, the source states that exemptions include "people who have to pay more than 8.13% of household income for insurance (taking into account any employer contributions or subsidies)" as well as those "whose incomes are so low that they are not required to file taxes" and others in the "Medicaid gap."

Source URL: https://www.kff.org/health-reform/issue-brief/the-cost-of-the-individual-mandate-penalty-for-the-remaining-uninsured/

### Was the penalty for not having health insurance under the ACA ever set at $10,000 for any individual?
Answer: The search result indicates that the maximum penalty under the ACA individual mandate was capped at the national average cost of a Bronze plan, and for a middle-income family of four in 2018, the penalty was $2,085—far below $10,000. There is no mention of any individual penalty ever being set at $10,000, so the claim is not supported by this evidence.

Source URL: https://www.healthinsurance.org/glossary/individual-mandate/

### Is it accurate to state that the Affordable Care Act penalizes people who cannot afford health care, rather than those who choose not to obtain insurance?
Answer: The search result indicates that the Affordable Care Act's individual mandate penalty applies to individuals who "do not obtain coverage," regardless of affordability, but includes exemptions specifically for those who cannot afford it, such as people whose costs exceed 8.13% of household income. Therefore, it is inaccurate to state that the ACA penalizes people who cannot afford health care, as the law provides exemptions for those facing genuine financial hardship; the penalty targets those who choose not to obtain insurance without meeting an exemption.

Source URL: https://www.kff.org/health-reform/issue-brief/the-cost-of-the-individual-mandate-penalty-for-the-remaining-uninsured/

### When was the individual mandate penalty under the ACA reduced to $0, and what law caused that change?
Answer: The individual mandate penalty under the ACA was reduced to $0 for years after December 31, 2018, by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017.

Source URL: https://www.hollandhart.com/2019-repeal-of-the-affordable-care-acts-individual-mandate-penalties

### What was the maximum possible penalty for not having health insurance under the ACA before the penalty was eliminated?
Answer: Based on the search result, the maximum possible penalty for not having health insurance under the ACA was capped at the cost of the national average premium for a bronze-level health plan for the relevant family size. For 2015, this cap was $207 per individual per month, up to $1,035 per month for a family of five or more, yielding a maximum annual penalty of $12,420.

Source URL: https://sgp.fas.org/crs/misc/R41331.pdf

### Does the Affordable Care Act require individuals to have health insurance or pay a penalty, or are there other mechanisms?
Answer: The Affordable Care Act (ACA) originally required individuals to have health insurance or pay a penalty (the individual mandate penalty), but the Tax Cut and Jobs Act of 2017 reduced that penalty to zero for years after 2018, effectively eliminating enforcement while technically leaving the requirement in place. As the source states, "beginning in 2019, the government will no longer attempt to collect the individual mandate penalties," and "without penalties there will be no enforcement and, in effect, no practical mandate to obtain coverage for 2019 and later years."

Source URL: https://www.hollandhart.com/2019-repeal-of-the-affordable-care-acts-individual-mandate-penalties

### What is the original source of the claim that someone who couldn't afford health care under the ACA gets penalized $10,000?
Answer: The search result does not contain sufficient information to identify the original source of the claim that someone who couldn't afford health care under the ACA gets penalized $10,000. The KFF summary of the Affordable Care Act describes the penalty for lacking coverage as "the greater of $695 per year up to a maximum of three times that amount ($2,085) per family or 2.5% of household income," with no mention of a $10,000 penalty.

Source URL: https://www.kff.org/health-reform/fact-sheet/summary-of-the-affordable-care-act/

### Did the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 affect the ACA's individual mandate penalty, and if so, how?
Answer: Yes, the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 affected the ACA's individual mandate penalty by reducing it to zero for years after 2018, effectively eliminating enforcement of the penalty. As the source states, "the individual mandate penalty has been reduced to zero for years after 2018" and "beginning in 2019, the government will no longer attempt to collect the individual mandate penalties."

Source URL: https://www.hollandhart.com/2019-repeal-of-the-affordable-care-acts-individual-mandate-penalties

### Under the Affordable Care Act, were individuals who faced financial hardship (e.g., insurance costing more than a certain percentage of income) exempt from the penalty?
Answer: Yes, the Affordable Care Act provided exemptions from the penalty for individuals facing financial hardship, including those who had to pay more than 8.13% of their household income for health insurance (taking into account any employer contributions or subsidies). The source states that exemptions apply to "people who have to pay more than 8.13% of household income for insurance (taking into account any employer contributions or subsidies) and certain individuals who have membership in certain groups or face a particular hardship."

Source URL: https://www.kff.org/health-reform/issue-brief/the-cost-of-the-individual-mandate-penalty-for-the-remaining-uninsured/

## Final Judgement
The evidence clearly shows that the Affordable Care Act (ACA) provided exemptions from the individual mandate penalty for individuals who could not afford health insurance, such as those whose insurance costs exceeded 8.13% of household income. Additionally, the penalty for not having insurance was never set at $10,000 for an individual; the maximum penalty for an individual was capped at the national average cost of a Bronze plan, and for a family of five or more in 2015 it could reach $12,420, but not for a single person. By 2020, the penalty had been reduced to $0 due to the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017. Therefore, the claim that someone who couldn't afford health care under the ACA gets penalized $10,000 is explicitly contradicted by the evidence.

`refuted`

### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The Affordable Care Act (ACA) provided exemptions from the individual mandate penalty for those who could not afford coverage, such as when insurance costs exceeded 8.13% of household income ([KFF](https://www.kff.org/health-reform/issue-brief/the-cost-of-the-individual-mandate-penalty-for-the-remaining-uninsured/)). The penalty for an individual was never set at $10,000; the maximum for a single person was capped at the national average cost of a Bronze plan, and even for a family of five or more in 2015 it could reach $12,420 ([FAS](https://sgp.fas.org/crs/misc/R41331.pdf)). By the 2020 tax year, the penalty had been reduced to $0 by the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act of 2017 ([Holland & Hart](https://www.hollandhart.com/2019-repeal-of-the-affordable-care-acts-individual-mandate-penalties)). Thus, the claim is contradicted on multiple grounds.